1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,611
Right, Sandra.

2
00:00:01,611 --> 00:00:05,900
You wanted to see me to get some feedback on your group's proposal.

3
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:10,580
The one you're submitting for the Geography Society field trip competition.

4
00:00:10,580 --> 00:00:15,293
I've had a look through your proposal and I think it's a really good

5
00:00:15,293 --> 00:00:15,630
choice.

6
00:00:15,630 --> 00:00:19,879
In fact, I only have a few things to say about it,

7
00:00:19,879 --> 00:00:25,897
but even in an outline document like this you really have to be careful to avoid typos

8
00:00:25,897 --> 00:00:28,376
and problems with layout in the proposal,

9
00:00:28,376 --> 00:00:30,500
and even in the contents page.

10
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:34,570
So read it through carefully before submitting it, okay?

11
00:00:34,570 --> 00:00:35,540
Will do.

12
00:00:35,540 --> 00:00:40,638
And I've made a few notes on the proposal about things which could have

13
00:00:40,638 --> 00:00:41,730
been better sequenced.

14
00:00:41,730 --> 00:00:42,690
Okay.

15
00:00:42,690 --> 00:00:46,284
As for the writing itself, I've annotated the proposal as

16
00:00:46,284 --> 00:00:49,160
and where I thought it could be improved.

17
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:56,373
Generally speaking, I feel you've often used complex structures and long sentences for the sake of it

18
00:00:56,373 --> 00:00:58,070
and as a consequence..,

19
00:00:58,070 --> 00:01:03,544
although your paragraphing and inclusion of sub- headings help.., it's quite hard to follow

20
00:01:03,544 --> 00:01:05,890
your train of thought at times.

21
00:01:05,890 --> 00:01:08,380
So cut them down a bit, can you?

22
00:01:08,380 --> 00:01:09,280
Really?

23
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:09,330
Yes.

24
00:01:09,330 --> 00:01:13,850
And don't forget simple formatting like numbering.

25
00:01:13,850 --> 00:01:16,140
Didn't I use page numbers?

26
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:18,224
I didn't mean that.

27
00:01:18,224 --> 00:01:22,391
Look, you've remembered to include headers and footers,

28
00:01:22,391 --> 00:01:23,954
which is good,

29
00:01:23,954 --> 00:01:27,080
but listing ideas clearly is important.

30
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:28,970
Number them or use bullet points,

31
00:01:28,970 --> 00:01:30,230
which is even clearer.

32
00:01:30,230 --> 00:01:33,880
Then you'll focus the reader on your main points.

33
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:38,666
I thought your suggestion to go to the Navajo Tribal Park was a very

34
00:01:38,666 --> 00:01:39,350
good idea.

35
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:41,920
I've always wanted to go there.

36
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:46,600
My father was a great fan of cowboy films and the Wild West

37
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:51,640
so I was subjected to seeing all the epics, many of which were shot

38
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:52,000
there.

39
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,008
As a consequence, it feels very familiar to me and it's awesome both geographically

40
00:01:58,008 --> 00:01:58,867
and visually,

41
00:01:58,867 --> 00:02:02,300
so it's somewhere I've always wanted to visit.

42
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:08,210
The subsequent research I did and the online photographs made me even keener.

43
00:02:08,210 --> 00:02:08,773
Interesting.

44
00:02:08,773 --> 00:02:14,400
Right, let's look at the content of your proposal now.

45
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:16,890
Did you find it comprehensive enough?

46
00:02:16,890 --> 00:02:18,760
Well, yes and no.

47
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:22,968
You've listed several different topics on your contents page,

48
00:02:22,968 --> 00:02:26,240
but I'm not sure they're all relevant.

49
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:26,648
No?

50
00:02:26,648 --> 00:02:32,355
Well, I thought that from the perspective of a field trip, one thing I

51
00:02:32,355 --> 00:02:36,840
needed to focus on was the sandstone plateaux and cliffs themselves.

52
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:41,360
The way they tower up from the flat landscape is just amazing.

53
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:45,791
The fact that the surrounding softer rocks were eroded by wind

54
00:02:45,791 --> 00:02:49,820
and rain, leaving these huge outcrops high above the plain.

55
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:53,720
It's hardly surprising that tourists flock to see the area.

56
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:57,890
Well, yes, I'd agree with including those points...

57
00:02:57,890 --> 00:03:01,564
And then the fact that it's been home to native American Navajos

58
00:03:01,564 --> 00:03:04,320
and all the social history that goes with that.

59
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:09,850
The hardships they endured trying to save their territory from the invading settlers.

60
00:03:09,850 --> 00:03:14,370
Their culture is so rich - all those wonderful stories.

61
00:03:14,370 --> 00:03:16,544
Well, I agree it's interesting,

62
00:03:16,544 --> 00:03:20,457
but it's not immediately relevant to your proposal, Sandra,

63
00:03:20,457 --> 00:03:25,240
so at this stage, I suggest you focus on other considerations.

64
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:29,960
I think an indication of what the students on the trip could actually do

65
00:03:29,960 --> 00:03:32,994
when they get there should be far more central,

66
00:03:32,994 --> 00:03:37,040
so that certainly needs to be included and to be expanded upon.

67
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:41,140
And I'd like to see something about the local wildlife,

68
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:45,650
and vegetation too, not that l imagine there's much to see.

69
00:03:45,650 --> 00:03:48,950
Presumably the tourist invasion hasn't helped.

70
00:03:48,950 --> 00:03:53,640
Okay, I'll do some work on those two areas as well.

71
00:03:53,640 --> 00:03:59,350
But you're right, there's not much apart from some very shallow-rooted species.

72
00:03:59,350 --> 00:04:04,119
Although it's cold and snowy there in the winter, the earth is baked

73
00:04:04,119 --> 00:04:07,788
so hard in the summer sun that rainwater can't penetrate.

74
00:04:07,788 --> 00:04:11,090
So it's a case of flood or drought, really.

75
00:04:11,090 --> 00:04:12,392
So, I understand.

76
00:04:12,392 --> 00:04:18,468
Now, before we look at everything in more detail, I've got a few factual

77
00:04:18,468 --> 00:04:19,770
questions for you.

78
00:04:19,770 --> 00:04:24,768
It would be a good idea to include the answers in your finished proposal,

79
00:04:24,768 --> 00:04:26,910
because they're missing from your draft.

80
00:04:26,910 --> 00:04:27,710
Fine.

81
00:04:27,710 --> 00:04:30,852
So, you mentioned the monoliths and the spires,

82
00:04:30,852 --> 00:04:32,030
which was good,

83
00:04:32,030 --> 00:04:35,172
but what area does the tribal park cover?

84
00:04:35,172 --> 00:04:36,350
Do you know?

85
00:04:36,350 --> 00:04:38,300
12,000 hectares,

86
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:46,100
and the plain is at about 5,850 metres above sea level.

87
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:48,531
Larger than I expected.

88
00:04:48,531 --> 00:04:49,139
Okay.

89
00:04:49,139 --> 00:04:51,570
Where's the nearest accommodation?

90
00:04:51,570 --> 00:04:54,500
That's a practical detail that you haven't included.

91
00:04:54,500 --> 00:04:56,720
Have you done any research on that?

92
00:04:56,720 --> 00:04:57,206
Yes.

93
00:04:57,206 --> 00:05:01,092
There's nowhere to stay in the park itself,

94
00:05:01,092 --> 00:05:05,950
but there's an old trading post called Goulding quite near.

95
00:05:05,950 --> 00:05:08,260
All kinds of tours start from Goulding, too.

96
00:05:08,260 --> 00:05:09,820
What kind of tours?

97
00:05:09,820 --> 00:05:14,128
Well, the most popular are in four-wheel drive jeeps -

98
00:05:14,128 --> 00:05:17,000
but I wouldn't recommend hiring those.

99
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,435
I think the best way to appreciate the area would be to hire horses instead

100
00:05:23,435 --> 00:05:25,580
and trek around on those.

101
00:05:25,580 --> 00:05:31,581
Biking is not allowed and it's impossible to drive around the area in private

102
00:05:31,581 --> 00:05:32,010
vehicles.

103
00:05:32,010 --> 00:05:34,280
The tracks are too rough.

104
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:38,450
Okay, lastly, what else is worth visiting there?

105
00:05:38,450 --> 00:05:40,541
There are several caves,

106
00:05:40,541 --> 00:05:44,200
but I haven't looked into any details.

107
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:46,160
I'll find out about them.

108
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:47,184
Okay, good.

109
00:05:47,184 --> 00:05:50,770
Now what I'd like to know is..
